; A defect worth knowing about, not a design choice.
;
; The string parser reads a run of literal characters, then one escape, and
; loops. It stops as soon as a run comes back empty — so an escape only works
; when at least one ordinary character sits in front of it. That makes three
; perfectly ordinary strings into syntax errors:
;
;   "\"quoted\""        starts with an escape
;   "a\\\\b"              two escapes in a row
;   "a\"\"b"              likewise
;
; Each is in errors/ as its own case. This file holds the shapes that do
; work, so the boundary is visible from both sides.
2026-01-01 event "one escape after text" "a\"b"
2026-01-01 event "escape at the end" "a\""
2026-01-01 event "two escapes with text between" "a\"b\"c"
